A major hurdle to researchers in the field of protein design is the high degree of cooperativity that is the defining feature of globular protein structures. This cooperativity originates in part from the large number of stabilizing contacts between residues that are distant in sequence, which results in very complex topologies and likely serves to make protein structures robust and to prevent them from unravelling under the challenging conditions of the cell. In this respect, the structures of so-called ‘tandemrepeat proteins’ are strikingly different from those of globular proteins. They comprise tandem arrays of small structural motifs (20–40 amino acids) that pack in a linear fashion to produce elongated, one-dimensional architectures with a continuous hydrophobic core and extended solvent-accessible surfaces (Figure 1). Stabilizing contacts are made solely between residues close in sequence, either within a repeat or in adjacent repeats, and there are no direct contacts between distant parts of the polypeptide chain. A quantitative measure of the topological simplicity is given by the relative contact order (CO). CO is the average sequence distance between all pairs of contacting residues normalized by the total sequence length [1]. Typical COs for repeat proteins are in the range 0.05–0.07 (equating to sequence separations of the order of 6–9 residues) whereas the COs of globular proteins are in the range 0.1–0.2 (equating to sequence separations of the order of 10–30 residues). The regular, modular and one-dimensional nature of repeat protein structures makes it especially straightforward to dissect and redesign their stability, folding and molecular recognition properties (Figure 2).
Tandem-repeat proteins are ubiquitous in nature. They include ankyrin (ANK), tetratricopeptide (TPR), leucinerich (LRR), HEAT (Huntington, elongation factor 3, protein phosphatase 2A, and yeast kinase TOR1) and armadillo repeats, and their biological function is in binding other proteins (and, less commonly, small molecules and nucleic acids) interactions. The individual units of repeat proteins are too small for them to be independently folded, and, reflecting this feature, they are found in nature in arrays of at least three units; arrays can be as long as 20 units or more. These proteins are therefore distinct from repeat proteins such as titin, which are composed of tandem arrays of much larger, independently folded, globular domains and which we will not discuss here. We will also not discuss those repeat proteins that form circular closed structures (e.g. the $\upbeta$ - propeller and the $\upbeta$ -trefoil); these have been reviewed recently elsewhere [2]. The structures of the individual units in tandem-repeat proteins are such that the array forms a superhelix, and the geometry of the intra-repeat and inter-repeat packing results in a characteristic curvature and twist of the superhelix that is different from each subtype [3]. ANKs and TPRs are typically found in small arrays (typically 4–7 repeats), and leucine-rich, armadillo and HEAT repeats are found in much larger arrays (typically 10–15 repeats).
## Dissecting and resculpting the thermodynamic properties of repeat proteins
A consensus-design approach has been used with great success to construct artificial repeat proteins and to engineer new binding functions into them. In this approach it is assumed that within one family, the positions important for maintaining the fold are highly conserved and will therefore be identifiable easily by sequence alignment, whereas the positions involved in
