the binding of an individual family member to its specific target will not be conserved. No explicit design of the interrepeat interfaces is needed, indicating that the favourable inter-repeat interactions are encoded in the consensus sequence. The consensus-designed units are self-compatible and the artificial proteins constructed from them have been found to be very stable. Consensus-designed ANK proteins [4,5], which together with TPR proteins [6] were the first to be constructed, show very high expression levels in Escherichia coli, are monomeric and extraordinarily stable (up to 20 kcal mol1 or more, compared with $5-$ $10\mathrm{\bar{kcal}m o l}^{-1}$ for most globular proteins and for natural repeat proteins). These have been followed by consensusdesigned LRR, ARM and HEAT repeats [7–9]. Capping units are required to improve the solubility of repeat proteins, and they are different from the internal units as one face is exposed to the solvent rather than being packed against another unit. In $\ensuremath{\alpha}$ -helical repeat proteins such as ANKs, the capping units have the same structure as the repeats themselves, whereas in repeat proteins containing $\upbeta$ structure (e.g. LRRs and $\upbeta$ -helices [10]) one end of the repeat array, at least, tends to be capped by an $\upalpha$ -helical element (see, e.g. Figure $^{1\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}}$ ). The caps were found to be the Achilles heel of the otherwise extremely stable consensus-designed ANK proteins; they were, however, subsequently optimized by using computational modelling to pinpoint and correct the destabilising interactions [11]. It is noteworthy that an exact primary structure symmetry does not appear to result in misfolding of consensus-designed repeat proteins, unlike repeat-domain proteins such as titin, where low sequence similarity between adjacent domains have been shown to prevent misfolding [12], and certain classes of circular repeat proteins, where asymmetry appears to be required for efficient folding [13].
The features that distinguish the structures of repeat proteins from those of globular proteins are regularity, modularity and the absence of interactions between residues that are distant in sequence, and repeat proteins therefore constitute simple systems with which to dissect the principles underlying protein folding and stability. Chemical-induced unfolding monitored by conventional techniques, such as fluorescence and circular dichroism, typically yields denaturation curves that appear two-state
Schematic showing the modular assembly process that can be used to build novel repeat proteins with predictable properties. Artificial repeat proteins can be constructed with desired global properties by rational design of the individual units. For example (a) repeat proteins can be designed to be small or large; (b) repeat proteins can be designed to unfold in a fully cooperative manner or in a multi-step manner [15,31,34]; (c) the pathway of unfolding of repeat proteins can be designed, for example, to start from the N-terminus or from the C-terminus [30,38]; (d) repeat proteins can be designed or screened for novel binding capabilities [62,79], and they can be made multi-functional and multivalent [80].
(i.e. a single, highly concerted transition is observed, indicating that only the folded and unfolded states are populated and there are no partly folded intermediates) for small repeat proteins comprising up to seven repeats. Two parameters are obtained from fitting chemicalinduced unfolding data to a two-state model: the midpoint of unfolding and the m-value, a constant that is proportional to the change in solvent exposure upon unfolding; the m-value is therefore an indicator of the cooperativity of the transition [14]. Low m-values have, been observed for larger repeat proteins, indicative of multi-state unfolding behaviour. This breakdown in cooperativity occurs between ${\sim}100$ and 200 amino acids, equivalent to three to six ANKs or TPRs (although, by applying the principles detailed above, it is possible to extend this cut-off considerably). This limit in the cooperativity of repeat-protein structures is similar to that observed for globular proteins.
One-dimensional Ising models, which are highly appropriate for analysing repeat-proteins, have been used with great success to explain their unfolding behaviour at equilibrium. Ising models treat each repeat as an independent folding unit, and they have been used to rationalize observed deviations from two-state behaviour in terms of intermediates with certain repeats folded and other unfolded. They were applied originally to the ANK domain of Notch $[15^{\bullet}]_{\cdot}$ , and subsequently to consensusdesigned ANK and TPR proteins [16]. Using these models, the Regan group was able to engineer the sequences of consensus-designed TPRs so that the intrinsic repeat stability was enhanced without altering the inter-repeat stability, and they showed that the effects of the mutations on the denaturation curves could be predicted from the model [17]. Likewise, using Ising models Main and co-worker showed that introducing mutations that stabilized the inter-repeat interface increased the overall cooperativity of the TPR protein [18]. Wolynes, Komives and co-workers used Ising models to rationalize the variation in m-values across sets of wild type and mutant ANKs and TPRs in terms of the weakening of inter-repeat interactions upon mutation [19].
How does the distribution of stability across the repeat array determine the extent of folding cooperativity? Just like for globular proteins, two-state behaviour requires the constituent subdomains to all be of similar intrinsic stabilities with strong interactions between them. Weak inter-subdomain interactions or subdomains of very different intrinsic stabilities lead to the decoupling of repeats and hence multi-state behaviour $[19,20,21,22^{\bullet}]$ . Are repeat proteins as robust as globular proteins in terms of their resistance to fraying or partial unfolding of their structures? Wolynes, Komives and co-workers have proposed that natural repeat proteins are ‘poised’ at particular ratios of inter-repeat and intra-repeat interaction energies to allow them to readily undergo partial unfolding in physiological conditions and thereby direct their biological functions [19]. Indeed, a heterogeneous stability distribution, resulting in the ability to unfold a subset of repeats while maintaining the other repeats in a folded state, has been shown to facilitate molecular recognition by repeat proteins, post-translational modifications of repeat proteins, including phosphorylation [23] and hydroxylation [24], their degradation $[25^{\bullet}]$ and their translocation between different cellular compartments [26,27]. For PR65/A, the giant 15-HEAT-repeat scaffolding subunit of the protein phosphatase PP2A, we recently showed that there are five subdomains that alternate between high and low stabilities (Tsytlonok M, Craig P, Sivertsson E, Serquera D, Perrett S, Best R, Wolynes P, LSI, submitted for publication). Unfolded repeats enhance binding and catalysis. We have also proposed that this alternation of stability serves to protect aggregation-prone low-stability regions by the high-stability regions (Tsytlonok M, Sormanni P, Vendruscolo M, LSI, submitted for publication).
In summary, using the principles outlined in this section, it should be straightforward to rationally dial in the stability profile of a repeat protein, and such an approach could be exploited in the construction of folding-based sensors.
## Directing the (un)folding pathways of repeat proteins
The cooperative nature of globular protein structures makes it difficult to dissect their folding pathways; there may only be one snapshot of the reaction that is accessible by conventional techniques, namely the rate-determining transition state. Using protein engineering (so-called ‘phivalue analysis’) many folding transition states have been mapped and, despite the variety of different native structures, a common folding mechanism has emerged. According to the so-called ‘nucleation-condensation’ mechanism, the transition state is diffuse in structure, that is, all of the protein is involved to some extent. The involvement of the whole poplypeptide chain in this type of transition state prevents partial unfolding, which can otherwise lead to misfolding and disease. In contrast to globular proteins, repeat proteins tend to have rather polarized folding mechanisms, as shown by phi-value analysis of p16 [28], Notch [29], myotrophin [30], D34 $[31^{\bullet}]$ , Internalin B [32] and $\mathbf{I}\ltimes\mathbf{B}\ltimes$ [33]. Related to this, experiments on series of consensus-designed TPRs $\left[34,35^{\bullet},36\right]$ and ANKs [16] showed that the unfolding rates were slowed as the number of repeats was increased, whereas the refolding rates were unaffected. These data suggest that there is a common rate-determining step in the folding of all small-sized and medium-sized repeat proteins folding, involving a nucleus of 2 or 3 repeats (as predicted in an early computational study by Wolynes, Komives and co-workers $[22^{\bullet}])$ ; interestingly a recent phivalue analysis of $\mathrm{I}\ltimes\mathrm{B}\ltimes$ indicates that not all structural elements within a repeat necessarily fold concomitantly [33]. The one-dimensional growth of structure in repeatprotein folding can be understood in terms of a capillarity model [37].
The complexity (multiplicity of pathways and states) of repeat-protein folding results from the symmetrical nature of their structures. At the same time, however, the simple, one-dimensional nature of the structures enables us to dissect this folding complexity, even for giant repeat proteins, and to thereby visualise the energy landscape at a level of detail that would be impossible to achieve for many globular proteins. The distinctive features of repeat-protein folding are nicely illustrated by the changing behaviour of repeat proteins of increasing size (Figure 3a). The first distinctive feature, as described above, is polarized pathways. The second distinctive feature is the accessibility of multiple pathways of similar energy. Wolynes, Komives and coworkers predicted such behaviour $[22^{\bullet}]$ before it was first observed experimentally by Lowe and Itzhaki [30]. This degeneracy originates from the symmetry inherent in the structure of repeat protein. Folding starts at the most stable repeats, and consequently the folding pathway can straightforwardly be directed by manipulating the stabilities of individual repeats [30,38]. For the 12-ANK D34 [31] and the 7-ANK gankyrin (Hutton R, Lowe A, Wilkinson J, LSI, unpublished results), the upward curvature apparent in the unfolding limb (Figure 3b) can be rationalized in terms of parallel pathways. It is possible to redesign the folding mechanism to direct flux exclusively through one pathway, and the change in folding route is manifest in strikingly different shapes of the unfolding arms in the redesigned variants (Figure 3b). For the giant HEATrepeat protein PR65/A that comprises five subdomains, the pathway can potentially bifurcate along alternative routes at two points in the unfolding reaction, and the route taken can be altered by mutation (M. Tsytlonok, P. Craig, E. Sivertsson, D. Serquera, S. Perrett, R. Best, P. Wolynes, LSI, submitted for publication). Thus, both the equilibrium and the kinetics of unfolding of repeat proteins appear to be very sensitive to mutation; as there are many different states of similar energy, the ensemble can be altered easily, even by single, conservative mutations [14].
The third feature of the folding of repeat protein is manifest in a downward curvature in the urea dependence of the unfolding rate constants (Figure 3a). This behaviour can be rationalized by a mechanism in which unfolding occurs by gradual unpeeling of the structure from one end of the repeat array $[28,30,31^{\bullet},33]$ .
## Repeat proteins as elastic nanosprings
The lack of stabilizing contacts between units distant from each other in the primary structure is thought to result in greater flexibility of repeat proteins when compared with globular proteins, and it has been proposed that repeat proteins constitute a distinct class that lie midway between globular proteins and intrinsically disordered proteins [39,40]. Indeed, experimental studies, using atomic force microscopy (AFM), have shown that repeat proteins are elastic molecules [41]. An interesting example of how mechanics might direct the function of a
