repeat protein is PR65/A, the scaffold subunit of protein phosphatase 2A (PP2A). Molecular dynamics simulations suggest that force-induced fluctuations, in response to spindle tension, will cause catalytic and substrate-bound regulatory PP2A subunits, bound to opposite ends of PR65/A, to move relative to each other, thereby controlling the enzyme’s activity $[42^{\bullet}]$ . Both natural and consensus-designed repeat proteins are mechanically weak, unfolding under low forces of ${\sim}50\mathrm{pN}$ [43,44]. The mechanical unfolding of repeat proteins appears to be non-cooperative, occurring repeat by repeat, and the repeats are able to refold rapidly against residual force. Molecular dynamics simulations indicate that there are subtle differences in the mechanics of natural and designed ankyrin-repeat proteins and differences in the mechanical strength depending on whether their unfolding is forced to propagate from the N-terminus or Cterminus $[44,45^{\bullet}]$ . These studies also show how force modulates the binding of a repeat protein to its ligand and how the mechanical properties are modulated by binding $[45^{\bullet},46,47]$ . Helical repeat proteins show low mechanical fatigue during multiple stretching-relaxation cycles, although hysteresis is much greater for LRRs, which contain elements of beta structure [48]. The extension ratios associated with repeat-protein unfolding are very high (ratios of 10–15 for repeat proteins compared with 2–5 for globular proteins) and compare favourably with those of known elastomeric molecules (such as elastin, which also has a modular construction). Although not under an external force, time-dependent fluctuations observed for the ANK protein $\mathbf{I}\ltimes\mathbf{B}\ltimes$ , as measured by single-molecule FRET between fluorescent dyes, underscore the spring-like behaviour of these proteins [33]. These properties could potentially be exploited for the bottom-up construction of new biomaterials (also discussed below).
## Constructing versatile binding molecules from repeat-protein building blocks
The non-globular, elongated shapes of repeat proteins result in large and diverse surfaces for molecular recognition, enabling cooperative multivalent interactions (Figure 1). A range of different binding modes is employed; for example, those repeat proteins with large curvature and small twist (e.g. LRRs) form concave surfaces that can wrap around a globular protein domain. Those repeat proteins with large twist and small curvature (e.g. armadillo and HEAT repeats involved in nuclear transport) have surfaces with grooves that can bind to extended peptides. Early in the 2000’s it was recognised that the unique properties of these versatile binding proteins could be readily exploited as non-antibody scaffolds [49]. First, the residues important for binding are distinct from those important for stability of the fold, and hence consensus-designed proteins, with fixed framework positions and variable binding positions, can be used to construct novel binding molecules. Second, repeat proteins are modular, consensus-designed repeats are self-compatible, and there are no constraints of the number of modules that one can assemble; the interaction surfaces are consequently also modular and can be used in a combinatorial fashion — individually exchangeable or modifiable and put together in any order. Third, proteins constructed from consensus-designed repeats express in very high yields and can be extraordinarily stable, both important characteristics for biomedical and biotechnology applications. Fourth, in contrast to antibodies, repeat proteins have the potential to provide binders for larger surfaces and could thereby act as inhibitors of protein-protein interactions. A number of artificial ANK proteins directed against enzymes and multi-protein complexes have indeed been shown to act in this way [50–52]. Here, we summarise developments within the last few years in consensus-designed ANKs (DARPins), TPRs (CTPRs), armadillo repeat proteins (ArmRPs) and TALE repeat proteins, and we describe their uses as novel binding partners for a range of applications that include anti-cancer and anti-viral therapeutics. In almost all cases novel binding capabilities have been incorporated by using randomized libraries and ribosome or phage display, rather than by rational design.
DARPins have dominated the field to date. Most of the libraries used comprise an N-cap, a C-cap, and two or three randomized internal repeats. Examples include a DARPin against vascular endothelial growth factor A VEGF-A with the highest reported affinity that has been obtained from a naı¨ve library without additional randomization [53]. This DARPin was injected into mouse eyes and penetrated directly to the retina, which not only demonstrates their efficacy in targeting retinal disease but also proves their potential in ophthalmic diagnostics and drug delivery, offering the first steps towards the next generation of ophthalmic biologics. DARPins have also been developed as microbicides that protect against HIV entry. DARPins that target CD4, a glycoprotein that is the primary receptor for HIV, were selected from ribosome display. They were found to bind in a highly specific manner to CD4 and they thereby inhibited HIV infection without affecting cell viability. The high stability and low production costs that are inbuilt characteristics of designed repeat proteins, mean that DARPins are promising candidates for further advancement in this area [54]. DARPins have been developed for intracellular inhibition of viral pathogens such as HIV-1 assembly, targeting the fused matrix and capsid domains of the HIV-1 Gag precursor [55]. $\mathrm{{Ank}^{G A G}\mathrm{{ID4}}}$ , comprising three internal modules flanked by N-terminal and C-terminal capping modules, was shown to bind to the N-terminal region of the capsid domain. When $\mathrm{\Delta\bk^{GAG}I D4}$ was stably expressed in cells, the production of HIV-1 was significantly reduced.
DARPins have also been used to investigate intracellular signalling. DARPins were selected from libraries that selectively bound to either the doubly phosphorylated (active) or unphosphorylated (inactive) form of mitogenactivated protein kinase ERK2 (extracellular signalregulated kinase 2). The DARPins were able to detect conformational changes in the kinase and, thereby, specifically monitor kinase activity in real time within the cell [56]. DARPins have been modified for use as reagentless fluorescent biosensors by coupling a solvatochromic fluorophore to a variable residue that is not important for target binding [57]. Here, a solvatochromic fluorophore was coupled to randomized cysteine mutants within the DARPin binding interface that did not interfere with target binding. The modified DAPRin could potentially provide a readout of its target without the requirement for manipulation of the biological sample or the addition of potentially disruptive reagents.
In parallel with these experimental studies, molecular dynamics simulations have been used to interrogate DARPin-ligand binding interfaces. For the DARPin off7 and its target maltose binding protein, these predictions revealed a hot-spot motif [58], which was subsequently confirmed experimentally by site-directed mutagenesis. It is thought that hot spots for one ligand could be a useful starting point for identifying interactions other binding partners.
Inhibitors of Hsp90 have been engineered using CTPRs [62]. Hsp90 chaperone is required for the maturation of many oncogenic proteins and is therefore an important cancer therapeutic target. TPR1 and TPR2a are two independent TPR domains from Hsp organizing protein (HOP), a protein required the assembly of Hsp70 and Hsp90; TPR1 binds to Hsp70 and TPR2a binds to Hsp90. Regan and co-workers designed a 3-TPR protein, CTPR390, in which binding residues from TPR2a were grafted onto the consensus TPR scaffold onto their scaffold before optimizing the sequence further in the protein $\mathrm{CTPR390+}$ . CTPR $390+$ was shown to be bind to Hsp90 with higher affinity and specificity than the TPR2A from which it was derived. Moreover, it successfully competes with Hop for binding Hsp90 in cells, inhibiting Hsp90 and, thereby, cell proliferation. Moreover, a 12-resdue peptide based on TPR2 had Hsp90 inhibitory activity, and when fused to a cell-penetrating peptide it was found to induce apoptosis in cancer cells without affecting nontumorous cells [63].
Regan, Hochstrasser and co-workers designed a 3-CTPR library and employed split GFP reassembly to screen the library for specific binding to a peptide from Dss1, a human protein that interacts with the tumour suppressor protein BRCA1 [64]. They then showed that the identified CTPRs inhibited the activity of the Dss1 protein in yeast, and they proposed that this strategy could be generally applied as a method for tracking or rewiring cellular pathways.
Much less well characterized are designed HEAT-repeat [9] and armadillo-repeat proteins (ArmRPs). ArmRPs are composed of tandem armadillo repeats of 42 amino acid residues and they their binding partners tend to be bound in an extended conformation (Figure 1). The superhelical ArmRP wraps around the binding partner, which is oriented in the opposite N-terminal to C-terminal direction, with each repeat contacting two amino acids of the binding partner. Pluckthun and co-workers recognised that it should be possible to develop individual repeats that bind specific two-residue sequences, and using consensus armadillo scaffolds that are self-compatible one could then assemble a stable ArmRP to recognise a long peptide sequence. After optimization of the consensusdesigned sequence including the use of molecular dynamics simulations, a stable library was constructed with an N5C format, which consisted of three randomized repeat modules flanked either side by one consensus repeat and N-terminal and C-terminal capping repeats at the respective termini $[65,66,67^{\bullet}]$ . From this library a highly specific binder against the peptide neurotensin was selected, although in the absence of a crystal structure the anticipated canonical mode of binding remains to be confirmed.
The extraordinary molecular recognition properties of repeat proteins also extend to nucleic acid targets. Transcription activator-like effectors (TALEs) bind effectorspecific DNA sequences and transcriptionally activate plant gene expression. TALEs comprise ${\sim}34$ -residue motifs that each binds one target base in the host genome with base specificity determined by two variable amino acids, the repeat variable diresidue (RVD). A designed TALE was shown to bind to the forward DNA strand of a specific DNA-RNA duplex [68]. Although the TALE repeats do not bind directly to the RNA strand, they surround it in such a way as to protect the phosphodiester backbone from degradation by RNAses, thereby opening the door for potential therapeutic intervention against HIV and other retroviruses via the TALE system. Exploiting the modular nature of TALE binding to DNA, TALE nucleases (TALENs) have been constructed, similar to zinc finger nucleases (ZFNs). These are made by fusing the catalytic domain of FokI to TALE modules custom combined to target a unique stretch of genomic nucleotide sequence. They have a number of advantages over ZFNs in terms of base recognition, and although only recently discovered, they have already been used very successfully for gene targeting in numerous contexts $[69^{\bullet},70,71]$ . Analogous to designer TALErepeat proteins for DNA, PUF (Pumilio and FBF homology) proteins have been designed with predictable sequence-specific RNA binding properties [72,73].
## The chemistry and material science of repeat proteins
Repeat proteins are emerging as potentially interesting building blocks for the construction of novel functional biomaterials. Specifically, the modular architecture, high stability, low production costs and built-in binding capabilities make them ideal for this purpose. CTPR have been used to create into stimuli-responsive hydrogels $[74^{\bullet},75]$ . One component of this two-component system is a protein made up of several TPR modules, with or without peptide-binding capability; the other component is a PEG-peptide cross-linker comprising a four-armed star-like PEG coupled to a short TPR-binding peptide. When the two components were mixed, gel-like networks were produced. Gel properties such as morphology and functionality are encoded by the individual repeat building blocks and by the cross-linking PEG-peptides. Moreover, since the TPR-peptide interface was stabilized by electrostatic interactions, the gels could be strengthened by decreasing the ionic strength or weakened by increasing the ionic strength. The authors illustrate how these hydrogels can be used in various applications, for example as a cell matrix and for drug delivery.
Native chemical ligation has also been used to synthesize LRR and TPR proteins from individual modules [76,77] and to assemble CTPR into superhelical filaments $[78^{\bullet}]$ . The termini of the CTPRs were designed such that native chemical ligation resulted in the formation of a peptide bond between the N-terminal thiolate group of one protein and the C-terminal thioester group of another protein. The filaments produced were rigid up to approximately $30\mathrm{nm}$ before they gained a degree of flexibility and extended up to microns in length. Over time, the individual fibres formed complex heterogeneous networks. Therefore, further development of the system to enable the controlled assembly of the CTPRs will prove important in the future.
## Summary
The success of the DARPin technology is reflected in the fact that they have already entered clinical trials. What does the future hold for other aspects of repeat-protein engineering? Studies published to date already show that the one-dimensional nature of repeat proteins allows one to map the energy landscape to a degree that would be impossible for globular proteins. Another consequence of this one-dimensionality is that certain properties are likely to be much more apparent in longer rather than shorter repeat arrays. Therefore, despite the challenges associated with the study of such large proteins (the folding of ${\sim}300{\-}600$ residue repeat proteins have recently been characterized), the rewards should make the effort worthwhile. Finally, the first few studies are beginning to show how one can extend the modular design process involved in the creation of individual repeat proteins to the assembly of repeat proteins into novel biomaterials that exploit their built-in molecular recognition functionalities.
